# REDMARS LAN HTTPS front door (task 7.2).
#
# Static self-signed cert (scripts/gen-lan-cert.sh -> ./certs/), NOT Caddy's
# own `tls internal` on-demand issuer. That issuer's GetCertificate callback
# sends a fatal TLS "internal_error" alert the instant a ClientHello carries
# an ALPN extension — which is every real client (curl, Node, browsers all
# offer h2/http1.1 ALPN by default). Verified across curl, Node's https, and
# Windows schannel: all three fail identically against `tls internal`, all
# three work against a plain loaded cert. See gen-lan-cert.sh for the retest
# note if a future Caddy version is worth trying again.
#
# The cert has no CA of its own to install separately — it IS what each LAN
# client trusts directly (see docs/https-lan.md for the one-time per-client
# install step; that step, not this file, is what actually kills the browser
# warning).
#
# Two ports under one host rather than one path-routed site: the web app
# calls its API via a full origin (VITE_API_URL), not a same-origin `/api/*`
# prefix, and the API sets no global prefix — path-based routing would
# collide with the SPA's own client-side routes (both the app and the API
# have a `/reports` path, for one). Splitting by port avoids touching either
# app to add one.
#
# host.docker.internal reaches the dev servers running on the host (apps/api,
# apps/web are not containerized yet — that's task 7.13). Once they are, this
# should point at their container names on the compose network instead.
# Vite's dev server must be started with --host or host.docker.internal can't
# reach it (it binds loopback-only otherwise).

:443 {
	tls /certs/redmars.crt /certs/redmars.key
	reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:5173
	log
}

:8443 {
	tls /certs/redmars.crt /certs/redmars.key
	reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:3000
	log
}
